WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.550
- Not even one year ago, I was a resident.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.950
Now there's a TV show called The Resident.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.072
Let's get started.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.655
(upbeat music)

00:00:11.681 --> 00:00:14.320
(classical music)

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.970
- It is true, surgeons
play their favorite music.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.360
- Oh yank that sucker.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.980
- When they're operating.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.560
- Did you guys know that
this is my first surgery

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.620
with doctor Bell?

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.670
- No kidding, we have to get a photo.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:26.610
- Make it quick.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.443
- Get in, Chu.

00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:30.170
- Okay, no, no one's
taking out their cellphone

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.440
in the middle of an
operation and taking selfies.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.794
- I wish we could get
one without the mask.

00:00:35.794 --> 00:00:37.131
- Quit clowning, Chu.

00:00:37.131 --> 00:00:38.240
- Uh oh, tremor?

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.910
- Just one more, I think we got it.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.793
- I'm going to send this to my mother.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:47.558
- You'll get us all fired,
cameras aren't allowed in the OR.

00:00:47.558 --> 00:00:50.595
- He's waking up, I need to up the C-roll.

00:00:50.595 --> 00:00:51.428
- Oh, oh.

00:00:51.428 --> 00:00:52.261
- Oh my god.

00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:53.094
- Did you hit an artery?

00:00:53.094 --> 00:00:54.290
- On an appendectomy?

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.247
- You're losing blood fast.

00:00:56.247 --> 00:00:57.370
- I need two liters of normal saline

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.203
wide open

00:00:58.203 --> 00:00:59.036
Call for four units of blood

00:00:59.036 --> 00:01:00.203
and do FFP STAT.

00:01:03.437 --> 00:01:04.270
- I can suction.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.680
- He's breathing very
heavily, rightfully so.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:07.630
- You've got to clamp something,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.580
he's lost at least two liters already.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.570
- Come on! Come on!

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.830
- What splattered on him
may be a quarter of a liter,

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.880
two liters is a lot of blood,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.310
and that's probably not two liters.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.880
He has some shaky hands,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.110
may have hit an artery.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:20.943
When you hit an artery,

00:01:20.943 --> 00:01:22.070
it does splash like that.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.010
The first step during a surgery

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.930
is to get one of the
tools called the Hemostat.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.700
And basically, it clamps off the artery

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.200
to get it to stop bleeding.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.164
- CPR isn't going to put all
that blood back into his body.

00:01:33.164 --> 00:01:34.339
- Whoa.

00:01:34.339 --> 00:01:35.839
- Don't die on me!

00:01:38.816 --> 00:01:39.649
- Happy?

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.960
I'm assuming this is someone's bad dream,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.790
because they're not
following proper protocol.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.940
I have no idea why he's stopping CPR.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.263
Nothing makes sense here.

00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:53.305
- He's so... dead.

00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:54.138
(flatline noise from machine)

00:01:54.138 --> 00:01:54.971
- Time of death

00:01:54.971 --> 00:01:56.310
- Okay, okay.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.143
Whoa.

00:01:57.143 --> 00:01:59.220
You guys told me The
Resident is the most accurate

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.320
medical show on television.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.670
So far,

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.410
this has been incredibly inaccurate.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.740
This gentleman's heart stops
in the middle of the procedure,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.290
because he was losing blood.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.577
They were supposed to get blood

00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:11.890
and start the blood flow through an IV.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.440
They started chest compressions.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.780
They didn't follow the
cardiac life support algorithm

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.960
of getting epinephrine,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.770
rechecking the rhythm after two minutes.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.870
And it looks like that
called the time of death

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.910
after 30 seconds.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.463
- Well, I think we can
all agree missed dose-

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.213
- What?

00:02:29.213 --> 00:02:30.323
- Let this unfortunate situation-

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:32.363
- What? You're kidding, right?

00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:34.230
- The patient woke up,
his arm hit my hand.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.740
- You left the blade in the field.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.573
You nicked the artery.

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:38.331
- Well, you never should have OK'ed him

00:02:38.331 --> 00:02:39.164
for surgery in the first place.

00:02:39.164 --> 00:02:39.997
His INR was abnormal.

00:02:39.997 --> 00:02:40.830
- The upper range of normal.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:41.663
That's never going to fly.

00:02:41.663 --> 00:02:42.496
- You know what, I'm-

00:02:42.496 --> 00:02:44.403
- We're all on the same team, here.

00:02:45.305 --> 00:02:46.138
Right?

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:48.363
- Oh my God.

00:02:48.363 --> 00:02:50.140
He's trying to blame it on the patient

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.000
coming in with a high INR,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.460
which is basically the
ability of the patient...

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.760
The inability of a
patient to clot properly.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.380
So, if you have a very high INR,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.770
you're more likely to bleed out.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.640
If this happened, I hope
that the people around me

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.880
have the courage to speak up

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.330
and say something about it.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:08.163
In fact,

00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:09.010
one of the biggest initiatives

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.620
that have been going on in hospitals

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.440
over the last ten to 20 years,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.240
is give nurses the voice and the courage

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.490
to speak up when they see doctors,

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.530
especially senior doctors
like this gentleman,

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.170
who's a Chief of Surgery,

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.920
who has been practicing for 30 years,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.267
to speak up and say

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:28.230
"No, you've made a mistake
and you need to own up to it,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.930
and figure out what went wrong,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.950
and how we can prevent
this in the future."

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:32.870
This is awful.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:33.970
This is an awful situation.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:35.513
I have goosebumps, honestly.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.016
- You have 206 bones,
and I can name each one.

00:03:39.016 --> 00:03:40.380
(laughs)

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.900
- It's a very cheesy
way to turn somebody on.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.010
- Everything you thought you
knew about medicine is wrong.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.083
All the rules you followed, we'll break.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:50.913
I have only one rule,

00:03:50.913 --> 00:03:52.370
covers everything,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.233
I'm never wrong.

00:03:54.083 --> 00:03:57.190
You do whatever the hell I tell you,

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.160
no questions asked.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.710
- I can't take this guy seriously.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.940
He sounds like he's from a Western movie,

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.410
and he's like, "Welcome
to the wild, wild, West."

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:07.930
Yes, in reality,

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.280
medical school is quite different

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.530
from life as a resident.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.730
There's a lot you think you know

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.920
about working in a hospital.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.130
When in reality,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.150
you start working in a residency,

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.200
you realize that you didn't know,

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.180
or what you thought you knew

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.130
was actually wrong,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.260
and you practice it in a different way.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.760
That's why those who get overly confident,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.490
by regurgitating facts and figures,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.850
really have their minds blown

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:31.980
when they enter the hospital,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.830
and they see the way
medicine is practiced.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.940
Because humans are very complex,

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.470
they don't present like
the way the textbook

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:39.970
says they will present.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.680
They don't always give
you a clear indication

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.600
of what's wrong with them.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.940
It's a lot more of a puzzle

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.400
and figuring out what's going on.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.260
The heart of what he's saying is true,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.670
the way he's presenting it

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.030
is way overblown and dramatic.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.610
- My last resident had an attitude, too

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:54.520
and do you know where he is now?

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.741
He's teaching eighth grade biology.

00:04:55.741 --> 00:04:56.574
- (laughs)

00:04:56.574 --> 00:04:57.407
- I cut him.

00:04:57.407 --> 00:04:58.410
You know what that means?

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.700
It means I can end your career,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:01.940
just like that,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.290
remove you from this residency,

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.350
at any time for any reason,

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.183
and if I do that,

00:05:05.183 --> 00:05:06.370
no other residency will take you.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:07.590
- Completely untrue.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.670
Senior residents don't have the ability

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:10.600
to get you kicked out,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.300
unless you do something,
just horribly wrong.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.380
And, if you lost your spot in a residency,

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.470
because you disagree
with the senior resident,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.250
it doesn't mean that no other
residencies will touch you.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.550
Again, a completely overblown statement.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.383
Untrue.

00:05:23.383 --> 00:05:24.903
I guess for the dramatic
factor of the show.

00:05:24.903 --> 00:05:26.350
- This is Dobroslav.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.640
He's Croatian, speaks no English.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.260
He has severe cauda equina syndrome.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.500
What are we worried about?

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:32.333
- Early paralysis.

00:05:32.333 --> 00:05:33.166
- Hey, man.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.430
- What's the first sign of paralysis?

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:35.263
- Anal tone.

00:05:35.263 --> 00:05:36.706
- Stick your finger up his ass.

00:05:36.706 --> 00:05:39.680
- (laughs)

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.840
- Normal procedure is to get an MRI.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:42.810
- Thank you so much

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.150
for telling me about normal procedure.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.580
- Cauda Equina Syndrome is where you have

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.756
severe narrowing of the
area of the spinal cord,

00:05:49.756 --> 00:05:51.840
where your nerves travel through.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.380
So, you lose sensation
of your lower limbs,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.330
you lose the ability to
have proper anal tone.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.890
Some people have incontinence,

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:03.320
where they just pass their bowels,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.900
they lose urinary control,

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.290
and just have urinary incontinence,

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.710
meaning that they pee themselves.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.170
And, if any of those things happen,

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.930
you have to call 911

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.150
because Cauda Equina...

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.440
This procedure... this condition
that they're talking about,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.790
is a medical emergency.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.340
Obviously one of the ways to test that,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.750
is to do a rectal exam,

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.030
and check the sphincter tone,

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.210
but he's being really
rude about it. (laughs)

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.683
- Good afternoon, we need
to explore your rectum.

00:06:26.076 --> 00:06:28.190
(translates to Croatian)

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:29.950
- Back in the day, we
used to have translators

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:31.210
that lived in the hospital...

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.750
I mean, worked in the hospital.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.310
Now, we have really good intercom systems,

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.460
some hospitals even have iPads

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.873
that connect you to another person,

00:06:39.873 --> 00:06:40.920
who can be the functioning translator.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.280
The correct way to do this,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.500
is to not talk to the translator
and have them translate it,

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.530
but talk to the patient, normally,

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:48.670
and have the translator

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.470
somewhere behind you, or on the phone,

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:52.050
talking to them, translating.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.330
So, you're still having a
conversation with the patient,

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.030
not a conversation with the translator.

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.173
That's a very important
distinction to make.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.210
I was hoping this show
wouldn't involve sex,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.530
but I'm striking out week by week.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.378
Because apparently,

00:07:11.378 --> 00:07:13.530
everyone's in love in the hospital.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.670
Maybe I've just worked
in the wrong hospitals.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:16.960
- Acute Leukemic on chemo,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.260
fiancee called because she
was shaking uncontrollably.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.030
- She spiked a fever of 100.8

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:26.320
- So, commonly someone who
has chemotherapy performed

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.130
on them, they can develop
something known as

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.670
neutropenic fever, it's
when a specific type

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.180
of white blood cells is very low,

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:33.053
and you have a fever.

00:07:33.053 --> 00:07:34.890
It's a very dangerous situation.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.500
Broad spectrum antibiotics,

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.430
meaning antibiotics
that cover a whole host

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.410
of different bacteria,

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.600
need to be given right away

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.500
in order to prevent the person from dying,

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:45.530
because their immune system

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.600
is incapable in dealing
with bacteria on its own.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.600
So, I think this is a pretty
interesting case, already.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.170
And I've just seen like
five seconds of it.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.003
- There was some vomiting,

00:07:53.003 --> 00:07:54.010
there was no blood in it,

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.310
last chemo was a week ago.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.090
- Hey.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:58.923
- You're here.

00:07:58.923 --> 00:08:00.640
- Very accurate presentation so far,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.320
knowing when the last chemo treatment's

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.490
very important, when judging

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.760
what the next step of
the treatment plan is.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.074
- I'm scared.

00:08:08.074 --> 00:08:11.340
- You're running a fever,
it's just another infection.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.160
Chemo's still crushing your immune system,

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.231
we'll get you started on
broad spectrum antibiotics,

00:08:16.231 --> 00:08:18.631
again, and acetaminophen
to get your fever down.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:20.800
Get cultures from both arms,

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:21.969
urine,

00:08:21.969 --> 00:08:22.802
she'll need a head CT.

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:23.635
- Okay.

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:26.430
- Don't worry, we'll
get this under control.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:27.820
Get you both back home soon.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.010
- Having a good rapport
with patients like that,

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:31.410
is very important.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:35.300
Nurses, and some doctors,
even people that are just

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.610
spending time in the hospital,

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:37.750
for short periods of time,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.860
are very somber when
they're around sick people.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.290
Especially chronic sick people,

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:42.950
who have been sick for
a long period of time.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:43.783
But in reality,

00:08:43.783 --> 00:08:45.800
they would love for someone to come in

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.860
with a little more lighthearted approach,

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.000
can laugh with them,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.010
make them smile.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:52.700
I'll always try and
have a laugh with them,

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.620
and tell some jokes,

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.620
especially if I know the family well.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.362
And, I think that makes a
very unpleasant experience

00:08:59.362 --> 00:09:01.230
a little bit more bearable.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:02.400
That's just my take on it.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.280
- How'd you get that cheeseburger, Chet?

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:05.775
- Delivery app.

00:09:05.775 --> 00:09:06.660
- (laughs)

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.030
- Looks like you haven't
been following your diet.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:10.520
- Diets don't work.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.126
- Have you been taking your insulin?

00:09:12.126 --> 00:09:14.548
- I don't want a lecture, Nic.

00:09:14.548 --> 00:09:15.381
- What?

00:09:15.381 --> 00:09:17.983
- I'm here 'cause my toe is killing me.

00:09:19.024 --> 00:09:21.940
- (gagging noise)

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:23.223
Severe gangrene.

00:09:26.685 --> 00:09:27.518
- (laughs)

00:09:27.518 --> 00:09:29.870
So, a gangrene... this is really gross.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.073
A gangrene in his toe,

00:09:31.941 --> 00:09:33.380
could be so infected and dead,

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:34.520
that it's basically necrotic,

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:36.198
that means dead tissue.

00:09:36.198 --> 00:09:37.843
Then it can fall off like that.

00:09:37.843 --> 00:09:38.676
Obviously it's a little exaggerated.

00:09:38.676 --> 00:09:41.780
The smell is probably the
worst part of all of it,

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.020
because the bacteria,

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.750
once they eat your tissue,

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:46.670
they release a very foul smell.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.530
It will light up the entire room.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:49.363
I'm not talking about,

00:09:49.363 --> 00:09:50.380
you have to sniff the wound.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:51.680
As soon as you walk into the room,

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:52.740
and there's gangrene present,

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:53.573
you're going to smell it.

00:09:53.573 --> 00:09:54.819
That is very true.

00:09:54.819 --> 00:09:56.094
- (screaming)

00:09:56.094 --> 00:09:57.080
- Settle down!

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:58.094
- Calm down!

00:09:58.094 --> 00:09:58.927
- I need you to look at me.

00:09:58.927 --> 00:10:01.040
- New admission. 21 year old girl,

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.060
history of IV drug use.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.510
Likely endo.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:05.910
- She was trying to steal Dilaudid,

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:07.290
now she wants to leave AMA.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.900
- She's been spiking fever, vommitting.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:10.230
- She's using again, isn't she?

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:11.490
She took all my money-

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.570
- Those who use drugs,

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.120
especially injection drugs,

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.800
they're predisposed to a
whole host of illnesses.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.170
So, this is a common
presentation, unfortunately.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.170
Especially in light of the opioid epidemic

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.410
that's going on right now.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:27.093
When you inject into your body, anything,

00:10:27.943 --> 00:10:28.860
especially in a non-sterile technique,

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.120
meaning the needle isn't clean,

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.260
your skin isn't clean,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:32.810
you're more presidposed to things like

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:34.890
meningitis, endocarditis.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.750
Meningitis is an infection of the pads

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:37.930
surrounding the brain.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:40.470
Endocarditis is an infection
of the heart valves.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.330
These are life-threatening illnesses

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.000
that can make you act this way,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.530
because bacteria is
festering in your body.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.800
And unless it's treated
quickly and correctly,

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:50.700
you can die.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.390
And that's just talking
about an infection.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.330
Think about all the other
things that could be going on.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.780
When you're under the influence of drugs,

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.920
it's very possible that
you're acting this way,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.530
as a result of an overdose,

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.720
from simply the drug.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.900
But when a patient comes
into the emergency room,

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.680
and they're presenting
with this kind of outcry,

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:06.590
screaming,

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.010
what we call altered mental status, AMS.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:09.843
We have to figure out,

00:11:09.843 --> 00:11:10.960
is it related to the drugs?

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.120
Is it because of an infection?

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:13.620
It is something more sinister?

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.470
Has this patient had a seizure?

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.050
There's a lot of things that are happening

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:19.040
simulatenously in a doctor's mind.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.430
So, it's not an easy
situation to deal with.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:24.470
But, ER doctors are the
frontline of dealing with it.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.620
And once the patient is stabilized

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.480
and ready to be admitted
into the hospital,

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.010
it then goes to internal medicine doctors,

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:31.843
like this gentlemen,

00:11:31.843 --> 00:11:33.930
or family medicine doctors, like myself.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:35.930
- If you walk out of here
without any antibiotics,

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.547
this will kill you.

00:11:37.547 --> 00:11:38.650
If you give us a chance,

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:39.750
we can save your life.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:45.643
- I'll stay, if you give me
three milligrams of Dilaudid.

00:11:47.214 --> 00:11:51.090
- (laughs) Two. If you calm down.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.420
- I can't say what he's doing is wrong,

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:57.960
because she's likely
withdrawing from Dilaudid,

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:59.410
or opioids, or heroin,

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.340
whatever it may be.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:01.620
In order to help her condition,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.720
it's possible that you
need to taper her off,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.933
meaning give her smaller
and smaller doses,

00:12:06.881 --> 00:12:07.714
more spaced apart,

00:12:07.714 --> 00:12:09.960
of the same chemical that
she normally gets high on.

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:12.860
Plus, if it's going to
make her reconsider,

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.330
and stay and get treated with antibiotics,

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:16.497
for her endocarditis,

00:12:16.497 --> 00:12:17.960
you're saving her life.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.620
Some people may disagree
with this approach,

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.650
and say, "Absolutely not,
she's not getting Dilaudid."

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.700
Some will say that there
is a medical benefit.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.700
So, that's why practicing
medicine is an art,

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:27.533
it's not a science,

00:12:27.533 --> 00:12:29.630
because two doctors can look
at at the same situation

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.198
and have different
approaches for salving it.

00:12:32.198 --> 00:12:33.031
I understand what he's doing,

00:12:33.031 --> 00:12:34.239
and I sort of respect it.

00:12:34.239 --> 00:12:35.072
Oh.

00:12:35.072 --> 00:12:35.905
- Chloe, baby!

00:12:35.905 --> 00:12:37.453
- Get a crash cart!

00:12:37.453 --> 00:12:38.900
- I'm not getting a pulse

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:39.733
- Code blue-

00:12:39.733 --> 00:12:41.613
- Get them out of here!

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:44.820
- Someone falls, and they have no pulse?

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:45.660
You call for help,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.000
and without even thinking about it,

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.724
you're pumping on the
chest, chest compressions.

00:12:48.724 --> 00:12:50.900
Chest compressions save lives.

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:52.360
I've said it before, chest compressions.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:54.540
I'm going to say it one more
time, chest compressions

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.080
is the first thing you do,

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.470
even if you have no training in it,

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.040
start pumping on the chest.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:12:59.930
- You're running the code.

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:00.835
- I've never done a code.

00:13:00.835 --> 00:13:01.960
- Do you want to amp up the Bicarb?

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:03.070
- He's in charge.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:04.840
- Page anesthesia.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:05.990
- When you're running a code blue,

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:06.840
you're following the

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.760
Advanced Cardiac Life Support Algorithm.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.960
It's literally written out for you.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:12.930
You give each person a role.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:13.890
You do chest compressions,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.030
you monitor the medications,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:15.870
you monitor the time,

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:16.830
you monitor the rhythm,

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:18.290
and everybody has roles.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:21.160
After that, there is a specific algorithm,

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.440
you literally follow steps on little cards

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.730
that you can carry in your pocket,

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:26.640
of when to recheck the rhythm,

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:27.980
what medications to give,

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.270
what options of medications do you have,

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:31.130
what dosage.

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.380
- What is the first
question you ask in code?

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.228
- Rhythm.

00:13:36.228 --> 00:13:37.128
What's the rhythm?

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:38.943
PEA.

00:13:38.943 --> 00:13:41.720
- PEA is Pulseless Electrical Activity.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:43.150
It basically means,

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:44.890
the heart has a rhythm,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:46.610
but you do not feel a pulse.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:48.560
There's some electricity
going through the heart,

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.393
but it's useless,

00:13:49.393 --> 00:13:53.090
because it's not creating
enough of a muscular motion,

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:53.950
within the heart,

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:55.000
to create a pulse,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.300
to make the heart beat.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:59.100
PEA is not a shockable rhythm.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.440
Meaning, you do not use
the paddles for that.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:02.720
You use epinephrine,

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:03.630
you use drugs,

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:04.690
you use chest compressions,

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.370
and you hope to get the patient back.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.100
And you wait for the rhythm to change,

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.420
into one of the two shockable rhythms.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:11.253
- Should we shock?

00:14:11.253 --> 00:14:12.087
- No, we can't.

00:14:12.087 --> 00:14:12.920
Her rhythm's not shockable.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:13.753
Give me one of epi.

00:14:13.753 --> 00:14:15.780
Make those compressions harder and faster.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:16.780
Prepare to intubate.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.140
- So, when you're doing
chest compressions,

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:19.973
you want to make sure

00:14:19.973 --> 00:14:21.720
you're doing quality chest compressions.

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.650
You want to push at least two
inches deep into the chest,

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.620
which sometimes can break ribs.

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.680
It's a horrible sound to hear,

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.680
but you're doing this to
help resuscitate the patient,

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.420
basically bring them back to life.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.180
So, if on the off chance you break a rib,

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.013
that's okay.

00:14:36.013 --> 00:14:37.610
It does happen in elderly folks,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.150
much more than in young folks.

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.830
You also want to do it to at least

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.250
100 beats per minute.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:43.083
So, if you think,

00:14:43.083 --> 00:14:44.350
there's 60 seconds in a minute,

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:45.810
you're pushing a little bit faster,

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:46.900
than once per second.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.420
A good way to sort of monitor
if you're doing it right,

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:50.630
is to sing the song,

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:51.940
in your head, please,

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:53.050
Stayin' Alive,

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.240
because that does go to about

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.240
100 beats per minute.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:56.924
It's the classic way that we're taught.

00:14:56.924 --> 00:14:59.527
(singing) Ah, Ah, Ah, Ah, Stayin' Alive!

00:15:00.484 --> 00:15:01.770
(laughs) Funny that it's
called Stayin' Alive,

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:04.350
and we're trying to bring
someone back to life.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.880
But, that's some of that raw,
medical edgy humor (laughs)

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.160
- It's been 24 minutes,

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:10.170
it's time to call the code.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:13.653
- No! This is my code,
you gave me this code.

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:21.650
- We've got a pulse.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:25.320
- You saved her life.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.500
- Doing a code on a young
person for 20 minutes

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:28.680
is not unrealistic.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:31.520
Also not unrealistic to
recommend stopping the code,

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:32.540
because the brain,

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:34.810
without oxygen for 24 minutes,

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.320
is obviously very dangerous,

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.170
and again,

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.580
even if you bring the pulse back,

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:39.790
will the brain work again?

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:41.860
You won't even know until
the person wakes up.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:43.730
The first line that one
of the other residents

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.030
tell him, is "You saved her life."

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:45.863
In reality,

00:15:45.863 --> 00:15:46.696
that could be a great thing.

00:15:46.696 --> 00:15:47.600
But also, on the other hand,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.100
it could also be an awful thing,

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.810
because she may just need
to be on a ventilator

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:53.060
for the rest of her life.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:54.450
Functionally brain dead.

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:56.420
Just her heart beating,
and her lungs working,

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:57.950
because she's on a machine.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:01.070
So... very difficult
situation to find yourself in.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:01.903
- You came in here,

00:16:01.903 --> 00:16:03.160
all bright and bushy-tailed,

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:04.290
ready to save lives.

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:05.950
But today you didn't save a life,

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.200
you saved a brainstem.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:09.770
You didn't listen to me.

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:12.880
- Did he do the wrong
thing? Not necessarily.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.352
In this situation, especially because

00:16:15.352 --> 00:16:16.640
it looked like their family was there,

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:18.350
this is a time to have a conversation

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.010
with the family, very quickly,

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:21.650
and explain to them what's going on.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:23.587
Explain the consequences of,

00:16:23.587 --> 00:16:24.737
"Hey, if we bring her back,"

00:16:24.737 --> 00:16:25.570
"at this point,"

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:26.457
"20 minutes in,"

00:16:26.457 --> 00:16:29.167
"she could come back with a pulse,"

00:16:29.167 --> 00:16:30.680
"but also be brain dead."

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:33.530
And help them decide what
to do in this situation.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:34.860
Allow them to make the decision.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:35.890
Because you know,

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:37.600
they're her next of living kin.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:40.110
Some hospitals have a cooling procedure,

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:43.415
that when someone undergoes
either a heart attack,

00:16:43.415 --> 00:16:46.630
or a sudden stoppage of the heart,

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:47.490
like she did,

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.300
that they cool the body down,

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:51.470
which slows the metabolic rate,

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.032
which can help the brain
survive a little bit longer.

00:16:54.032 --> 00:16:56.320
So, this doesn't always hold true.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:59.440
Don't use this as an
application for your own life,

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:01.530
or making decisions
for your family's life.

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:03.960
Treat each situation on its own.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:05.430
Talk to the doctors in front of you,

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.210
and make the best decision that you can,

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.060
with the information
given to you at the time.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.410
- What was rule one, Devon?

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.690
- Do whatever you tell me to do.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:16.370
No questions asked.

00:17:16.370 --> 00:17:17.840
- All we want to do is help our patients.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:20.120
But what they don't teach
us in medical school,

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.080
is there are so many ways to do harm.

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:23.930
- The first job of the
doctor is not to heal,

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:25.740
it's to first do no harm.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.940
Because if you look at the
history of doctors in the past,

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:29.770
we've made a lot of mistakes,

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:32.770
over treating patients,
under treating patients,

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.570
deciding what's right for our patients,

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:36.090
and going against their own wishes.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:38.030
I think we've done a lot
better in recent years,

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:40.360
but there's still plenty
of room to go to improve.

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.147
- If it were easy,
everyone would be a doctor,

00:17:42.147 --> 00:17:44.410
because this is the best job in the world,

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:45.913
despite everything.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:49.020
Because of everything.

00:18:18.314 --> 00:18:19.410
- There you have it.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.370
The Resident, season one,
episode one in the books.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:23.907
Initial impression...

00:18:23.907 --> 00:18:26.320
The show is absolutely ridiculous.

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:27.230
This resident,

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:29.730
while he's, you know, smart,

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.740
and has some experiences,

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:33.600
he just does some crazy things.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.800
He's a cowboy in my eyes.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.860
Deciding who lives and who dies.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:39.180
I'll say that they way
they're talking about the

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:40.300
medicine is accurate.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.450
Some of the medical terms that they use

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:43.580
are used accurately.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:45.960
The procedures,

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.830
somewhere in the middle...
50/50 of their accuracy.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.750
I think it's going to make for a fun show.

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:52.510
I definitely relate more to this show,

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.360
